{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli was born in 1140 and died in 1187 he was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death. He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. Raymond III was married twice first to Constance of Antioch and then to Eschiva of Saint Omer.\n\nRaymond III succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 when he was twelve years old. During his minority, the county was governed by a regency led by his mother Hodierna and his uncle Count Joscelin II of Edessa. In 1156, Raymond III came of age and assumed full control of the county.\n\nOne of the significant events during Raymond III's reign was the Battle of Harim in 1164. *Battle of Harim* Raymond III led a coalition of forces from the County of Tripoli, the Principality of Antioch, and the Kingdom of Jerusalem against the Zengid forces led by Nur ad-Din. The battle ended in a defeat for the Christians, and Raymond III was captured and imprisoned in Aleppo for nine years.\n\nRaymond III was released from captivity in 1173 following the intervention of the Byzantine Emperor Manuel I Komnenos. Upon his release, Raymond III returned to the County of Tripoli and resumed his rule.\n\nAnother significant event during Raymond III's reign was the *Third Crusade* which took place from 1189 to 1192. Raymond III played a prominent role in the crusade and provided military support to the leaders of the crusade, including Richard the Lionheart.\n\nRaymond III died in 1187 during the Battle of Hattin, which was a significant defeat for the Christians in the Holy Land. Raymond III's death marked the end of the Second Crusader States in the Levant.\n\nIn conclusion, Raymond III was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187. He led the County of Tripoli through significant events such as the Battle of Harim and the Third Crusade. Raymond III's death d